  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 243 PM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nSaturday - Sunday...\n\nThe center of the upper ridge retreats back toward the Red River \nValley Saturday and Sunday, and this will leave the Ohio River \nValley more in between the upper ridge and the stronger belt of \nzonal westerlies across the NE CONUS. This will continue to be a \nmostly dry and hot pattern for us, however we'll likely lose enough \nof the mid level subsidence inversion, and have a weak frontal \nboundary for a trigger (at least on Saturday) for some isolated to \nwidely scattered diurnally driven shower or storm chances. Focus \nshould mainly be across the NE half of the CWA for these time \nperiods, farther away from the upper ridge center.\n\nStill looking at temperatures in the low to mid 90s on Saturday with \ndew points also continuing to be in the upper 60s and low 70s so max \nafternoon heat indices in the upper 90s to around 100 will be \npossible. For Sunday, temperatures will be more solidly into the mid \n90s (perhaps even a few upper 90s across the SW), however dew points \nlook to drop off more into the 60s by this time which will provide a \nlittle relief from the triple digit max heat index values.\n\nMonday - Tuesday...\n\nWe'll remain in a similar upper pattern going into early next week \nwith the upper ridge centered back toward the Rio Grande, and a \nswath of zonal westerlies stretching from the Corn Belt eastward \nthrough the mid Atlantic. Some instability will still build over our \nregion on the NE periphery of that upper ridge, however we'll also \nbe in play for some weak shortwave activity passing through that \ncorridor of slightly enhanced upper flow mentioned above. The end \nresult will be increased chances for mainly diurnally driven showers \nand storms each day, but could get a little extra boost from any \npassing shortwave activity as well. \n\nIn addition to the increased shower and storm chances, it looks like \nwe also see a decent shot at some cooler air with the upper ridge \nlosing influence. ECM Ensemble temperature guidance still has some \nnotable spread in the data for Monday and Tuesday (10-15 degrees \nbetween the hi and low members!). For now, the latest NBM data leans \nmore toward the higher end of the guidance for Monday with most of \nour temps ranging in the upper 80s and low 90s. The NE CWA will \nlikely be notably cooler in the low to mid 80s. By Tuesday, the NBM \nleans more toward the cooler end of the guidance envelope with highs \nin the upper 70s and low 80s for most. The combination of increased \nshower and storm activity combined with the loss of the upper ridge \nseems to be the driving factor to these changes.\n\nWednesday - Thursday...\n\nUpper ridging tries to build back to the NE a bit through mid week. \nLower confidence on how much if any the upper ridge gets established \nback over our area, and the latest NBM temp data suggests that will \nbe delayed a bit. Will call for an overall drier forecast by this \ntime with highs peaking in the upper 70s and low 80s. These \ntemperatures will be values more close to normal for this time of \nyear.",
